WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.030
The river sensor by our school

2
00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.890
sends a reading every minute.

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.700
If we processed it in a nightly batch,

4
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.820
we'd learn about a flood the morning

5
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:09.200
after.

6
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.130
Today: batch and stream processing,

7
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.370
and how to choose between them.

8
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.190
Batch processing collects data and

9
00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.110
handles it together, on a schedule:

10
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.870
every hour, or every night.

11
00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.710
It's simple, cheap and easy to rerun.

12
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.530
But it's late by design.

13
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.460
Stream processing handles each event

14
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.520
as it arrives, within seconds.

15
00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.310
It's fast, but it runs all the

16
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.410
time, and it must cope with late,

17
00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.690
missing and duplicated events.

18
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.820
Streams are noisy: a

19
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.420
splash, a boat's wake.

20
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.760
So you average over a window, such

21
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.100
as the last five readings.

22
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.800
A bigger window hides more

23
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.760
noise, but reacts later.

24
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.880
On our river, one reading in the average

25
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.620
gives three false alarms from splashes.

26
00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.750
Five gives one, from a boat's wake.

27
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.140
With ten there are none, but the real

28
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.600
alert is five minutes late; with

29
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.640
thirty, fifteen minutes late.

30
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:16.330
Quick check!

31
00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.170
A flood alert, or a monthly attendance

32
00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.310
report: which one needs a stream?

33
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.860
The flood alert: minutes matter.

34
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.900
The monthly report is fine as a nightly

35
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.740
batch: cheaper, simpler, and easy to

36
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.120
rerun.

37
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.900
Real streams are messy.

38
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.300
Events arrive late or out of order,

39
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.340
so use the time they happened, not

40
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.160
the time they arrived.

41
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.160
And events can arrive twice, so

42
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.220
make processing idempotent too.

43
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.810
Many systems do both.

44
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.340
A stream drives the alert now, and a

45
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.040
nightly batch recomputes the day's numbers

46
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.460
carefully, from everything that arrived.

47
00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.490
In the app, handle the river

48
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.230
readings as a stream or in batches.

49
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.220
Change the window and the batch size, and

50
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.700
watch false alarms and delays trade off.

51
00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:11.450
Your turn.

52
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.580
I make the averaging window bigger.

53
00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:15.900
What happens?

54
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.280
Fewer false alarms, but later alerts:

55
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.820
choose by what each one costs.

56
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.430
Remember: batches are simple and late;

57
00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.510
streams are fast and always on.

58
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.880
Windows tame noise and add delay.

59
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.080
And many systems run both.

60
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.970
Try it tomorrow: list five systems

61
00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.710
you use, like bus times, exam

62
00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.310
results and a bank alert.

63
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.000
For each one, decide: batch

64
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.520
or stream, and why?
